  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A little bit cooler tomorrow inland, but \ngenerally remaining near to above normal through the upcoming \nweekend. Uncertainty remains for the potential for some rain \nthis weekend. \n\nLatest weather analysis reveals persistent upper-level ridging \nover the central Gulf coast, extending north into southeast \nCanada. Meanwhile, another upper trough continues to dig across \nthe Pacific Northwest into the northern Rockies. At the surface,\na weak and dry cold front has pushed through the area this \nafternoon, with 1034+ mb surface high pressure centered over \nOntario and Quebec. Temperatures are largely in the low to mid \n70s inland, while onshore flow brings a slightly cooler but \nstill seasonable afternoon along the coast with readings well \ninto the 60s to near 70.\n\nThe high behind the front slides offshore of coastal New \nEngland late tonight and will ridge south into the Mid-Atlantic.\nResultant light onshore flow will lead to developing low- and \nmid-level clouds late tonight into Wednesday morning, bringing \npatchy fog mainly along and west of I-95 within a few hours of \nsunrise Wednesday. The low clouds will linger for a good part of\nthe morning inland; have accordingly knocked temperatures down \na few degrees west of I-95. Meanwhile, along the coast and \nacross much of the Delmarva, a good amount of sunshine will \nbring another pleasant day with highs right around 70 degrees.\n\nThe surface ridge eventually anchors off the southern New \nEngland to Mid-Atlantic coast Wednesday night and Thursday, \nwhile low pressure remains suppressed well south near the \nBahamas. With high pressure sliding offshore, the building SSW \nflow allows for a warmer end to the week, with highs potentially\nreaching the mid to upper 70s Thursday and Friday before \ncooling slightly with increasing clouds on Halloween.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remaining dry this week. Uncertainty remains \nfor the potential for some rain late this weekend into early\nnext week.\n\nDry conditions are forecast to prevail through Saturday as upper\nridging builds over the region. By Friday and into the weekend,\nanother northern stream shortwave traversing central Canada will\nflatten the upper pattern. Halloween continues to look dry,\npleasant, and mild with increasing clouds ahead of a weak cold front,\nallowing for optimal conditions for trick-or-treaters Saturday\nnight. Models continue to slow the approach of the lead shortwave\npushing into the region late Sunday into early next week, now\nlooking more likely on Monday. While the pattern may turn \nunsettled for next week, latest 12z/27 ensembles continue to \nhint at weak systems crossing the area during the first few days\nof November. There remains typical spread among the probabilistic\nmodels regarding rain chances, with the EPS and GEPS ensemble a\nbit more bullish on QPF than is the GEFS. While welcome rain \nappears possible next week, none of the available guidance shows\nany promising prospects for drought-busting precipitation.",
